Fiveserver
==========
Copyright (C) 2011-2021 juce and reddwarf
License: BSD-style



ABOUT
=====


Fiveserver is a pure-Python implementation of network server for the
following games:

Pro Evolution Soccer 5 
Winning Eleven 9
Winning Eleven 9 Liveware Evolution
Pro Evolution Soccer 6
Winning Eleven 2007


Notable features:

* Full support for network play, including 2-vs-2 for PES6
* Persistent accounts and full game statistics, stored in MySQL database
* Administrative web-interface
* REST api to retrieve live stats



INSTALL
=======


The following two-step installation sequence should work as is on any Unix OS,
such as Linux (any flavour), FreeBSD or Mac OSX. If you are using Windows, then
it is recommended that you download a pre-built Windows-specific package from 
http://sites.google.com/site/fiveservercom/

1. Python 3 and dependencies
If you are on modern Debian or Ubuntu Linux then this should install everything
you need:

    sudo apt-get -y update
    sudo apt-get install python3 python3-venv python3-dev gcc make

Otherwise, you will need to use your OS tools to install:

    * Python 3.11 or newer
    * gcc or clang (for the optional asyncmy wheel build)
    * make


2. Return to fiveserver source directory and install Python environment and
packages by issuing this command (as yourself, not root):

    make install

This will create an isolated Python environment and install all Python packages
needed to run Fiveserver/Sixserver



CONFIGURE MYSQL DATABASES
=========================


The following instructions assume that you can use a command-line mysql client 
utility to connect to your MySQL server with a root account.

For Fiveserver (PES5/WE9/WE9LE), you will need to create a database and grant
appropriate permissions. If you use default database name and login/password 
(you can change all of those in ./etc/conf/fiveserver.yaml), then it would be this:


    create database fiveserver;
    create user 'fiveserver'@'%' identified by 'we9le';
    grant select, insert, update on fiveserver.* to 'fiveserver'@'%';
    use fiveserver;
    source ./sql/schema.sql


For Sixserver (PES6/WE2007), you will need to create a database and grant
appropriate permissions. If you use default database name and login/password 
(you can change all of those in ./etc/conf/sixserver.yaml), then it would be this:

    create database sixserver;
    create user 'sixserver'@'%' identified by 'proevo';
    grant select, insert, update on sixserver.* to 'sixserver'@'%';
    use sixserver;
    source ./sql/schema6.sql



FIRST-RUN HTTPS SETUP (REQUIRED)
===============================

Release archives do not contain TLS private keys or certificates. Before starting
either service, provide a certificate and its matching unencrypted private key at
the files expected by `KeysDirectory` in `etc/conf/admin*.yaml`:

    etc/keys/serverkey.pem
    etc/keys/servercert.pem

Use your own PKI tooling or certificate authority to create and install them. For
local testing, a self-signed localhost certificate is acceptable; browsers will
show a trust warning. For public production, use a trusted certificate and include
intermediate certificates in `servercert.pem`. Restrict the private key to the
server account; on Unix use `chmod 600` on the key and `chmod 644` on the
certificate. Never commit or distribute the private key.

Create the same files under a custom `KeysDirectory` if your admin configuration
uses one.

Set database credentials in YAML or `FIVESERVER_DB_PASSWORD`, and set a strong
`FIVESERVER_ADMIN_PASSWORD` before exposing the service. Start from the project
directory so relative configuration, key and asset paths resolve correctly:

    python -m fiveserver five
    python -m fiveserver six

If startup reports missing TLS files, verify both paths and the working directory;
the server does not silently fall back to unencrypted admin HTTP or generate keys.
To renew a certificate, replace the pair after securing a backup and restart the
server.


USAGE
=====


Run one service directly from the project root:

    python -m fiveserver five
    python -m fiveserver six

Use a process manager such as systemd, a service wrapper, or Docker Compose for
background operation. The legacy `service.sh` helper has been removed.


## Docker

The root `Dockerfile` and `compose.yaml` run the asyncio implementation. The
project name is `fiveserver`, and each service has a fixed container name:
`fiveserver-five`, `fiveserver-six`, `fiveserver-five-db`, and
`fiveserver-six-db`. The image is built in two stages: an Alpine builder compiles
`asyncmy` and installs the package, then only the installed files are copied into
a clean Alpine runtime stage. The build tools and pip cache are not shipped in
the final image.

Compose also starts one MariaDB 11 container per service. The first start loads
`sql/schema.sql` into `fiveserver` and `sql/schema6.sql` into `sixserver`; the
named volumes `five-db-data` and `six-db-data` preserve them afterwards. The
server containers wait until their database reports healthy, and their own health
checks now verify both the registration HTTP endpoint and a live database query.

First complete the HTTPS setup above on the host. Compose mounts `etc/keys`
read-only into each container; an empty directory is not sufficient. Copy the
example environment file and replace every placeholder:

    cp .env.example .env

    # Edit .env:
    FIVESERVER_PUBLIC_IP=203.0.113.10
    FIVESERVER_FIVE_DB_PASSWORD=...
    FIVESERVER_SIX_DB_PASSWORD=...
    FIVESERVER_FIVE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=...
    FIVESERVER_SIX_ADMIN_PASSWORD=...
    FIVESERVER_ADMIN_BIND=127.0.0.1

    docker compose up --build

`.env` is ignored by Git. The admin HTTPS ports (`8181` for five and `8191` for
six) bind to `127.0.0.1` by default. Set `FIVESERVER_ADMIN_BIND=0.0.0.0` only
when remote admin access is intentional and protected.

The DB passwords above are used both for the MariaDB user and for the matching
server's `FIVESERVER_DB_PASSWORD`. The database names and users come from
`etc/conf/fiveserver.yaml` (`fiveserver`/`fiveserver`) and
`etc/conf/sixserver.yaml` (`sixserver`/`sixserver`); keep those matching if you
customize them.

The original `etc/conf`, `etc/data`, `etc/keys`, and `log` directories are
mounted into the containers. `etc/data/banned6.yaml` is the sixserver ban list;
`etc/data/banned.yaml` is the five-family list. Database data lives in Docker
volumes, so `docker compose down` keeps it. Use `docker compose down -v` when you
explicitly want to delete the databases and re-run the schema initialization.


## Logging

Log files use Twisted's original rotation defaults: rotate at **1,000,000
bytes**, then create numbered backups (`file.log.1`, `file.log.2`, ...). By
default the number of rotated files is unlimited, matching Twisted's
`maxRotatedFiles=None`.

Rotation is controlled by environment variables, not YAML:

```bash
FIVESERVER_LOG_MAX_BYTES=1000000
FIVESERVER_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT=
```

- `FIVESERVER_LOG_MAX_BYTES=0` disables rotation.
- `FIVESERVER_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT` unset or empty keeps unlimited backups.
- `FIVESERVER_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT=0` keeps only the newest rotated file.
- `FIVESERVER_LOG_BACKUP_COUNT=N` keeps `.1` through `.N`.

Docker Compose reads these from `.env`. Native runs must export them in the
server process environment.


## Testing

Runtime and development dependencies are declared in `pyproject.toml`; there is
no separate requirements file. Python 3.11 or newer is supported for local
installation and tests; the Docker image uses Python 3.13 by default.

    make install-dev
    .local/bin/python -m pytest -q
    .local/bin/python -m ruff check src tests
    .local/bin/python -m mypy src/fiveserver
    .local/bin/python -m pylint src/fiveserver --disable=all --enable=C0114,C0115,C0116
    .local/bin/python -m radon cc src/fiveserver -s -a

To audit only the runtime dependency set, use:

    make audit

This creates or refreshes a clean `.audit` virtual environment containing the
project's install dependencies plus `pip-audit`, then audits that environment.
Development tools such as pytest, pylint, ruff, mypy, and radon are deliberately
not installed there.

Set `FIVESERVER_TEST_DB_HOST` (and optional port/user/password) to run the
disposable MariaDB and startup integration tests.
Certificate-generation and HTTPS tests require OpenSSL. They generate temporary
TLS pairs rather than depending on deployment keys in the repository or ZIP.

MySQL integration and live-game-client checks require external services and are
not run by the default test suite.
